From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tom Englund <tomenglund26@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 19:13:20 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] nvidia-drm: add the content type connector property

KWin, like other compositors, sets the standard "content type" connector
property (graphics, photo, cinema or game) so that HDMI TVs can switch to
a matching picture mode, for example a low latency game mode. The value
goes into the AVI InfoFrame: the IT content bit and the CN1:CN0 content
type. nvidia-drm didn't have the property, and NVKMS always sends "IT
content, graphics" for CTA modes.

KWin sets the property in every page flip commit, not only in modesets,
so a change must not need a modeset, or the commit would fail. Add a
display attribute, NV_KMS_DPY_ATTRIBUTE_CONTENT_TYPE, rather than a field
in the flip request, which would change the ioctl ABI that NVIDIA's
userspace uses. NVKMS applies it to the AVI InfoFrame it builds, and when
it changes on an active HDMI display, sends the InfoFrame right away the
way a color range change does: a new core FID, so that flip-synchronized
InfoFrames switch slots, then the InfoFrame update and a kickoff. Its
default keeps what NVKMS chose before, so NVKMS clients that don't set it
see no change.

KAPI takes the value with a changed flag in the head's modeset config and
sets the attribute on the head's displays for every commit that has the
flag, modeset or flip; TEST_ONLY checks skip it, as it can't make a commit
fail. nvidia-drm attaches the property to HDMI connectors, like i915, and
passes it when it changes and with every modeset, so it also reaches a
display that is newly driven by the CRTC or was recreated by a hotplug.

Like on the other drivers, "No Data" is the property's default and clears
the IT content bit; KWin sets "Graphics" by default.

Not tested on HDMI hardware (none here). On DisplayPort the attribute is
only stored, as NVKMS sends no AVI InfoFrame there.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
